# 8 Essential statistics with NHANES

This source-faithful BioLang port follows every section of [*Biological Data Science with R*, Chapter 8: Essential statistics](https://bdsr.stephenturner.us/stats.html), from 8.1 through 8.7.3, in the original single-page order. Select **Prepare all** first: Studio downloads the two exact source-hosted files and verifies their sizes and SHA-256 checksums. All analytical figures are generated by BioLang from those files; no published image or synthetic replacement data is used.

## 8.1 Our data: NHANES

### 8.1.1 About NHANES

NHANES combines interviews, examinations, and laboratory measurements. The chapter uses a 5,000-person resampled teaching extract; reproduce the chapter with this table, but use the original survey design and weights for population inference.

### 8.1.2 Import and inspect

```biolang
let nh = read_csv("nhanes.csv")
let dictionary = read_csv("nhanes_dd.csv")
{people: nrow(nh), variables: ncol(nh), dictionary_rows: nrow(dictionary)}
```

The source uses the 5,000-person, 32-variable resampled NHANES teaching table. It is appropriate for reproducing the lesson, but it is not a replacement for survey-weighted analysis of the original NHANES sample.

## 8.2 Descriptive statistics

> **Understanding descriptive statistics**
>
> **Use them when:** you need to describe the observations you actually have before making claims beyond them.
>
> **How to read them:** centre answers where typical values lie, spread answers how different they are, and shape shows asymmetry, tails, gaps, or multiple groups. No single summary replaces the others.

### Reproduce the source exercises

```biolang
let pulse = nh |> drop_null("Pulse") |> col("Pulse")
let diastolic = nh |> drop_null("BPDia") |> col("BPDia")
let age = nh |> col("Age")
let income = nh |> drop_null("Income") |> col("Income")
{
    mean_pulse: mean(pulse),
    diastolic_range: [summary(diastolic).min, summary(diastolic).max],
    age_quartiles: [quantile(age, 0.25), quantile(age, 0.5), quantile(age, 0.75)],
    income_variance: variance(income),
    income_standard_deviation: stdev(income)
}
```

These are the same calculations and the same source table used by the chapter exercises.

### 8.2.1 Missing data

> **Understanding missing values**
>
> **Use this check when:** any variable may be absent, unrecorded, inapplicable, or lost.
>
> **How to read it:** report both the full row count and the number contributing to each result. Missingness can bias a result when the reason a value is absent is related to the quantity being studied.

```biolang
let income_summary = summary(nh |> col("Income"))
{
    total_rows: nrow(nh),
    observed_income: income_summary.count,
    missing_income: nrow(nh) - income_summary.count,
    mean_observed_income: mean(income)
}
```

### 8.2.2 Exploratory data analysis

> **Understanding exploratory data analysis**
>
> **Use it when:** you need to see distributions, groups, unusual values, and relationships before selecting a formal model.
>
> **How to read it:** plots suggest what to investigate and reveal assumptions that numbers can hide. They do not supply a p-value and should not be mined until a preferred story appears.

#### Histograms

The source draws four `geom_histogram()` panels. BioLang regenerates each one from the exact NHANES rows with the same bin counts, grey fill, ggplot panel, and axis labels.

### BMI, 30 bins

```biolang
histogram(nh |> col("BMI"), {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "BMI", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Weight in kilograms, 30 bins

```biolang
let observed_weight = nh |> drop_null("Weight") |> col("Weight")
histogram(observed_weight, {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "Weight", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Weight converted to pounds, 80 bins

```biolang
let weight_pounds = observed_weight |> map(|value| value * 2.2)
histogram(weight_pounds, {
    bins: 80, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "Weight * 2.2", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Age, 30 bins

```biolang
histogram(age, {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "Age", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

#### Scatterplots: height and weight

The source first plots all available observations and colours points by gender. BioLang uses the same complete rows and ggplot hue ordering.

```biolang
let everyone_complete = nh |> drop_null("Height") |> drop_null("Weight") |> drop_null("Gender")
stats_relationship_plot(
    everyone_complete |> col("Height"), everyone_complete |> col("Weight"),
    {
        group: everyone_complete |> col("Gender"), fit: false, interval: "none",
        theme: "ggplot", title: "", x_label: "Height", y_label: "Weight",
        legend_title: "Gender", note: false, width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

It then filters to `Age >= 18` and adds one linear-model smooth per gender with the source's grey confidence ribbons.

```biolang
let adults_complete = nh |> filter(|row| row.Age >= 18) |> drop_null("Height") |> drop_null("Weight") |> drop_null("Gender")
stats_relationship_plot(
    adults_complete |> col("Height"), adults_complete |> col("Weight"),
    {
        group: adults_complete |> col("Gender"), interval: "confidence", confidence: 0.95,
        theme: "ggplot", title: "", x_label: "Height", y_label: "Weight",
        legend_title: "Gender", note: false, width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

```biolang
{
    complete_height_weight_gender: nrow(everyone_complete),
    complete_adult_height_weight_gender: nrow(adults_complete)
}
```

## 8.3 Continuous variables

The outcome in this section is numeric. The method changes with the number and type of predictors and with the assumptions the data can support.

```biolang
let adults = nh |> filter(|row| row.Age >= 18)
```

### 8.3.1 T-tests

> **Understanding two-sample t-tests**
>
> **Use them when:** a numeric outcome is compared between two independent groups and a difference in means is the scientific target.
>
> **How to read them:** the estimate is the observed mean difference; its confidence interval gives compatible effect sizes; the p-value measures conflict with a zero-difference model. Welch's version is safer when group variances or sizes differ.

```biolang
let age_gender = adults |> drop_null("Age") |> drop_null("Gender")
let female_age = age_gender |> filter(|row| row.Gender == "female") |> col("Age")
let male_age = age_gender |> filter(|row| row.Gender == "male") |> col("Age")
let age_test = ttest(female_age, male_age, {variance: "welch"})
age_test
```

The source reports (t=1.9122), 3697.2 degrees of freedom, and (p=0.05593). The exact source table gives the same values in BioLang.

The source then repeats the Welch test for BMI by diabetes and annual alcohol use by relationship status.

```biolang
let bmi_diabetes = adults |> drop_null("BMI") |> drop_null("Diabetes")
let bmi_diabetic = bmi_diabetes |> filter(|row| row.Diabetes == "Yes") |> col("BMI")
let bmi_not_diabetic = bmi_diabetes |> filter(|row| row.Diabetes == "No") |> col("BMI")
let bmi_diabetes_test = ttest(bmi_diabetic, bmi_not_diabetic, {variance: "welch"})

let alcohol_relationship = adults |> drop_null("AlcoholYear") |> drop_null("RelationshipStatus")
let alcohol_committed = alcohol_relationship |> filter(|row| row.RelationshipStatus == "Committed") |> col("AlcoholYear")
let alcohol_single = alcohol_relationship |> filter(|row| row.RelationshipStatus == "Single") |> col("AlcoholYear")
let alcohol_relationship_test = ttest(alcohol_committed, alcohol_single, {variance: "welch"})
{bmi_by_diabetes: bmi_diabetes_test, alcohol_by_relationship: alcohol_relationship_test}
```

These are unpaired tests because each group contains different people. Repeated measurements of the same biological unit require a paired or repeated-measures analysis.

### 8.3.2 Wilcoxon test

> **Understanding the Wilcoxon rank-sum test**
>
> **Use it when:** two independent groups have an ordinal or strongly non-normal outcome and an ordering comparison is meaningful.
>
> **How to read it:** the test works from ranks, so it asks whether one group tends to contain larger values. It is not automatically a test of medians unless the distribution shapes are suitably similar.

The source first shows that `AlcoholYear` is strongly non-normal. Its `geom_histogram()` uses the default 30 bins; BioLang generates the same histogram from the same adult rows, with the source labels and ggplot panel.

```biolang
histogram(adults |> col("AlcoholYear"), {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "AlcoholYear", ylabel: "count",
    width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

```biolang
let alcohol_relationship = adults |> drop_null("AlcoholYear") |> drop_null("RelationshipStatus")
let alcohol_committed = alcohol_relationship
    |> filter(|row| row.RelationshipStatus == "Committed") |> col("AlcoholYear")
let alcohol_single = alcohol_relationship
    |> filter(|row| row.RelationshipStatus == "Single") |> col("AlcoholYear")
let alcohol_rank_sum = wilcoxon(
    alcohol_committed, alcohol_single,
    {method: "normal", continuity: true}
)
alcohol_rank_sum
```

The rendered chapter prints (W=1067954) and (p=0.0001659). With tied ranks, current R and BioLang both retain the half rank and calculate (W=1067954.5); the p-value rounds to the published value.

### 8.3.3 Linear-model equivalence

> **Understanding linear models**
>
> **Use them when:** the average numeric outcome can be described as an intercept plus one or more predictor contributions.
>
> **How to read them:** each coefficient is a conditional average change under the fitted model. Inspect residual patterns, uncertainty, influential observations, and the observed predictor range before interpreting coefficients.

The source compares adult BMI by relationship status three ways. The equal-variance t-test, two-group ANOVA, and 0/1 indicator regression have the same p-value; the ANOVA F statistic is the squared t statistic.

```biolang
let bmi_relationship = adults |> drop_null("BMI") |> drop_null("RelationshipStatus")
let committed_bmi = bmi_relationship
    |> filter(|row| row.RelationshipStatus == "Committed") |> col("BMI")
let single_bmi = bmi_relationship
    |> filter(|row| row.RelationshipStatus == "Single") |> col("BMI")
let single_indicator = bmi_relationship |> col("RelationshipStatus")
    |> map(|status| if status == "Single" { 1 } else { 0 })

let equal_variance_t = ttest(committed_bmi, single_bmi, {variance: "equal"})
let two_group_anova = anova([committed_bmi, single_bmi], {variance: "equal"})
let indicator_model = lm(single_indicator, bmi_relationship |> col("BMI"))
{
    t_test: equal_variance_t,
    anova: two_group_anova,
    indicator_model: indicator_model
}
```

```biolang
assert abs(alcohol_rank_sum.u_group_a - 1067954.5) < 0.000000001, "tie-aware source Wilcoxon W"
assert abs(alcohol_rank_sum.p_value - 0.00016585513405613698) < 0.000000000001, "source Wilcoxon p-value"
assert abs(equal_variance_t.statistic - -1.5318893541999188) < 0.000000001, "source equal-variance t statistic"
assert equal_variance_t.df == 3552, "source t-test degrees of freedom"
assert abs(two_group_anova.f_statistic - 2.3466849935110443) < 0.000000001, "source ANOVA F statistic"
assert abs(indicator_model.intercept - 28.513425287356096) < 0.000000001, "committed-group mean"
assert abs(indicator_model.slope - 0.34132453135287805) < 0.000000001, "single-group coefficient"
assert abs(equal_variance_t.p_value - two_group_anova.p_value) < 0.000000000001, "t-test and ANOVA p-values"
"Exact source data, calculation, and runtime figure checks passed."
```

### 8.3.4 ANOVA

> **Understanding ANOVA**
>
> **Use it when:** a numeric outcome is compared across three or more independent groups and the first question is whether any group mean differs.
>
> **How to read it:** the omnibus p-value does not identify which groups differ. Planned contrasts or multiplicity-adjusted comparisons answer that second question; group plots show whether means are an adequate summary.

```biolang
let smoking_complete = adults |> drop_null("BMI") |> drop_null("SmokingStatus")
let never_bmi = smoking_complete |> filter(|row| row.SmokingStatus == "Never") |> col("BMI")
let current_bmi = smoking_complete |> filter(|row| row.SmokingStatus == "Current") |> col("BMI")
let former_bmi = smoking_complete |> filter(|row| row.SmokingStatus == "Former") |> col("BMI")
let smoking_anova = anova([never_bmi, current_bmi, former_bmi], {variance: "equal"})
let smoking_tukey = tukey_hsd([never_bmi, current_bmi, former_bmi])
{anova: smoking_anova, tukey: smoking_tukey}
```

The source ANOVA reports (F=16.99) with 2 and 3553 degrees of freedom and (p=4.54\times10^{-8}). Its Tukey comparisons show that current smokers differ from both never and former smokers, while former and never smokers do not differ significantly.

The source boxplot also displays people whose smoking status is missing, although that `NA` group is excluded from the fitted ANOVA. BioLang regenerates the same four-group classic-theme boxplot from the exact adult rows.

```biolang
let smoking_bmi = adults |> drop_null("BMI")
stats_group_plot(
    smoking_bmi |> col("BMI"), smoking_bmi |> col("SmokingStatus"),
    {
        theme: "classic", points: "outliers", missing_label: "NA",
        group_order: ["Never", "Current", "Former", "NA"],
        show_n: false, title: "", x_label: "SmokingStatus", y_label: "BMI",
        width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

### 8.3.5 Linear regression

> **Understanding regression lines**
>
> **Use them when:** you want the average change in one numeric variable associated with a change in another.
>
> **How to read them:** slope gives change per x-unit, the ribbon gives uncertainty in the mean line, and scatter around it shows unexplained variation. Do not extrapolate beyond the observed x range or translate association into causation without a design argument.

```biolang
let height_weight = adults |> drop_null("Height") |> drop_null("Weight")
let fit = lm(height_weight |> col("Height"), height_weight |> col("Weight"))
{complete_rows: nrow(height_weight), model: fit}
```

The fitted equation is `Weight = -73.7058963 + 0.9199614 * Height`, with (R^2=0.2034476), exactly matching the source data calculation.

#### Fitted relationship

BioLang regenerates the source's `geom_point() + geom_smooth(method="lm")` figure from the exact 3,676 complete adult observations.

```biolang
stats_relationship_plot(
    height_weight |> col("Height"), height_weight |> col("Weight"),
    {
        interval: "confidence", confidence: 0.95, theme: "ggplot",
        title: "", x_label: "Height", y_label: "Weight", note: false,
        width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

#### Extrapolation warning

The second source call keeps the same points and fit but extends the fitted x range toward zero. BioLang reproduces that full-range geometry and the original title.

```biolang
stats_relationship_plot(
    height_weight |> col("Height"), height_weight |> col("Weight"),
    {
        interval: "confidence", confidence: 0.95, fullrange: true, x_limits: [0, nil],
        theme: "ggplot", title: "Friends don't let friends extrapolate.",
        x_label: "Height", y_label: "Weight", note: false,
        width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

The extended line exposes why the negative intercept should not be interpreted outside the observed height range.

```biolang
assert nrow(height_weight) == 3676, "source complete adult rows"
assert abs(fit.intercept - (-73.70589629259628)) < 0.000000001, "source intercept"
assert abs(fit.slope - 0.9199613842262466) < 0.000000001, "source slope"
assert abs(fit.r_squared - 0.20344761074176798) < 0.000000001, "source R squared"
"Exact source model and runtime figures verified."
```

### 8.3.6 Multiple regression

> **Understanding multiple regression**
>
> **Use it when:** you need to estimate one predictor's association with a numeric outcome while accounting for other measured predictors.
>
> **How to read it:** each coefficient is conditional on the other included terms. A coefficient changing after adjustment can reveal confounding, but adjustment alone does not establish causation.

```biolang
let testosterone_rows = adults |> drop_null("Testosterone") |> drop_null("PhysActive")
let testosterone_no = testosterone_rows |> filter(|row| row.PhysActive == "No") |> col("Testosterone")
let testosterone_yes = testosterone_rows |> filter(|row| row.PhysActive == "Yes") |> col("Testosterone")
let activity_t_test = ttest(testosterone_no, testosterone_yes, {variance: "equal"})
activity_t_test
```

The source reports (t=-2.4298), 3436 degrees of freedom, and (p=0.01516). The matching one-predictor linear model follows.

```biolang
let testosterone = adults |> col("Testosterone")
let activity_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive")}), testosterone,
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: false}
)
activity_model.coefficients
```

Adding age removes the physical-activity association at the 0.05 threshold in the source model.

```biolang
let activity_age_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({
        PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive"),
        Age: adults |> col("Age")
    }), testosterone,
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: false}
)
activity_age_model.coefficients
```

Adding gender substantially reduces residual variation; the physical-activity coefficient is again significant. The labels use BioLang's explicit bracket notation for treatment contrasts, so `Gender[male]` and `PhysActive[Yes]` are the source's `Gendermale` and `PhysActiveYes` terms.

```biolang
let multiple_full_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({
        PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive"),
        Age: adults |> col("Age"),
        Gender: adults |> col("Gender")
    }), testosterone,
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: false}
)
{
    coefficients: multiple_full_model.coefficients,
    complete_rows: multiple_full_model.complete_rows,
    excluded_rows: multiple_full_model.excluded_rows,
    r_squared: multiple_full_model.r_squared,
    adjusted_r_squared: multiple_full_model.adjusted_r_squared
}
```

```biolang
assert abs(activity_t_test.statistic - -2.429815439145285) < 0.000000001, "source activity t statistic"
assert activity_t_test.df == 3436, "source activity t degrees of freedom"
assert abs(activity_model.coefficients[1].estimate - 19.267018611090293) < 0.000000001, "source activity coefficient"
assert abs(activity_age_model.coefficients[1].estimate - -0.8002814918848092) < 0.000000001, "source age coefficient"
assert abs(multiple_full_model.coefficients[1].estimate - -0.5903616430454974) < 0.000000001, "source adjusted age coefficient"
assert abs(multiple_full_model.coefficients[2].estimate - 385.1989414002692) < 0.000000001, "source male coefficient"
assert abs(multiple_full_model.coefficients[3].estimate - 9.274924731642368) < 0.000000001, "source adjusted activity coefficient"
assert multiple_full_model.complete_rows == 3438 && multiple_full_model.excluded_rows == 269, "source complete cases"
assert abs(multiple_full_model.r_squared - 0.6968929855756988) < 0.000000001, "source multiple R squared"
"Exact source calculations passed."
```

## 8.4 Discrete variables

Both the observed outcome and the explanatory labels in this section are categorical.

### 8.4.1 Contingency tables

> **Understanding categorical association tests**
>
> **Use them when:** both variables are categories and each independent observation contributes to one table cell.
>
> **How to read them:** chi-square compares observed with independence-expected counts; Fisher's exact test is useful for sparse 2x2 tables. Pair the p-value with proportions or an effect estimate because evidence strength is not effect size.

The gender-by-diabetes table uses the source's row and column order. The second table adds the same marginal totals shown by `addmargins()`.

```biolang
let gender_diabetes_rows = adults |> drop_null("Gender") |> drop_null("Diabetes")
let gender_diabetes = gender_diabetes_rows |> cross_tab(
    "Gender", "Diabetes",
    {row_order: ["female", "male"], col_order: ["No", "Yes"]}
)
let gd_no = gender_diabetes |> col("No")
let gd_yes = gender_diabetes |> col("Yes")
let gd_total = sum(gd_no) + sum(gd_yes)
let gender_diabetes_margins = table({
    Gender: ["female", "male", "Sum"],
    No: [gd_no[0], gd_no[1], sum(gd_no)],
    Yes: [gd_yes[0], gd_yes[1], sum(gd_yes)],
    Sum: [gd_no[0] + gd_yes[0], gd_no[1] + gd_yes[1], gd_total]
})
{counts: gender_diabetes, with_margins: gender_diabetes_margins}
```

```biolang
let gender_diabetes_total_proportions = table({
    Gender: ["female", "male"],
    No: [gd_no[0] / gd_total, gd_no[1] / gd_total],
    Yes: [gd_yes[0] / gd_total, gd_yes[1] / gd_total]
})
let gender_diabetes_row_proportions = table({
    Gender: ["female", "male"],
    No: [gd_no[0] / (gd_no[0] + gd_yes[0]), gd_no[1] / (gd_no[1] + gd_yes[1])],
    Yes: [gd_yes[0] / (gd_no[0] + gd_yes[0]), gd_yes[1] / (gd_no[1] + gd_yes[1])]
})
{overall_proportions: gender_diabetes_total_proportions, row_proportions: gender_diabetes_row_proportions}
```

```biolang
let gender_diabetes_chi = chi_square_contingency(gender_diabetes |> drop_cols("Gender"))
let gender_diabetes_fisher = fisher_exact(gd_no[0], gd_yes[0], gd_no[1], gd_yes[1])
{chi_square: gender_diabetes_chi, fisher_exact: gender_diabetes_fisher}
```

The source calls unshaded base-R `mosaicplot(xt, main=NA)`. BioLang regenerates its grey count-area geometry, category order, inter-cell gaps, outer labels, and no-title layout from the same table.

```biolang
mosaic_plot(gender_diabetes, {
    row: "Gender", layout: "base", title: "", subtitle: "",
    x_label: "Gender", y_label: "Diabetes", fill: "#BEBEBE",
    show_values: false, width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

The race-by-insurance workflow repeats the same table, row-proportion, chi-square, expected-count, and mosaic sequence.

```biolang
let race_order = ["Asian", "Black", "Hispanic", "Mexican", "Other", "White"]
let race_insured_rows = adults |> drop_null("Race") |> drop_null("Insured")
let race_insured = race_insured_rows |> cross_tab(
    "Race", "Insured", {row_order: race_order, col_order: ["No", "Yes"]}
)
let ri_no = race_insured |> col("No")
let ri_yes = race_insured |> col("Yes")
let race_insured_margins = table({
    Race: ["Asian", "Black", "Hispanic", "Mexican", "Other", "White", "Sum"],
    No: [ri_no[0], ri_no[1], ri_no[2], ri_no[3], ri_no[4], ri_no[5], sum(ri_no)],
    Yes: [ri_yes[0], ri_yes[1], ri_yes[2], ri_yes[3], ri_yes[4], ri_yes[5], sum(ri_yes)],
    Sum: [
        ri_no[0] + ri_yes[0], ri_no[1] + ri_yes[1], ri_no[2] + ri_yes[2],
        ri_no[3] + ri_yes[3], ri_no[4] + ri_yes[4], ri_no[5] + ri_yes[5],
        sum(ri_no) + sum(ri_yes)
    ]
})
race_insured_margins
```

```biolang
let race_insured_row_proportions = table({
    Race: race_order,
    No: [
        ri_no[0] / (ri_no[0] + ri_yes[0]), ri_no[1] / (ri_no[1] + ri_yes[1]),
        ri_no[2] / (ri_no[2] + ri_yes[2]), ri_no[3] / (ri_no[3] + ri_yes[3]),
        ri_no[4] / (ri_no[4] + ri_yes[4]), ri_no[5] / (ri_no[5] + ri_yes[5])
    ],
    Yes: [
        ri_yes[0] / (ri_no[0] + ri_yes[0]), ri_yes[1] / (ri_no[1] + ri_yes[1]),
        ri_yes[2] / (ri_no[2] + ri_yes[2]), ri_yes[3] / (ri_no[3] + ri_yes[3]),
        ri_yes[4] / (ri_no[4] + ri_yes[4]), ri_yes[5] / (ri_no[5] + ri_yes[5])
    ]
})
let race_insurance_chi = chi_square_contingency(race_insured |> drop_cols("Race"))
{row_proportions: race_insured_row_proportions, chi_square: race_insurance_chi, expected: race_insurance_chi.expected}
```

```biolang
mosaic_plot(race_insured, {
    row: "Race", layout: "base", title: "", subtitle: "",
    x_label: "Race", y_label: "Insured", fill: "#BEBEBE",
    show_values: false, width: 960, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### 8.4.2 Logistic regression

> **Understanding logistic regression**
>
> **Use it when:** the outcome has two categories and you need prediction or adjusted associations from several predictors.
>
> **How to read it:** coefficients are changes in log odds; exponentiating gives odds ratios conditional on the other model terms. Check calibration, separation, functional form, and whether an odds ratio answers the practical question.

The source explicitly releveled race so White is the reference. BioLang uses a stable sort key to put the White level first, then preserves the source factor order for the remaining levels. Sorting rows does not alter the likelihood; it only pins the treatment-contrast baseline.

```biolang
let race_model_rows = adults
    |> mutate("RaceOrder", |row|
        if row.Race == "White" { 0 }
        else if row.Race == "Asian" { 1 }
        else if row.Race == "Black" { 2 }
        else if row.Race == "Hispanic" { 3 }
        else if row.Race == "Mexican" { 4 }
        else if row.Race == "Other" { 5 }
        else { 6 }
    )
    |> arrange("RaceOrder")
let insured = race_model_rows |> col("Insured")
    |> map(|value| if value == "Yes" { 1 } else if value == "No" { 0 } else { nil })
let insured_by_race = stats_glm_diagnostics(
    table({Race: race_model_rows |> col("Race")}), insured,
    {family: "binomial", include_values: false}
)
insured_by_race.coefficients
```

```biolang
let insured_adjusted = stats_glm_diagnostics(
    table({
        Age: race_model_rows |> col("Age"),
        Income: race_model_rows |> col("Income"),
        SleepHrsNight: race_model_rows |> col("SleepHrsNight"),
        Race: race_model_rows |> col("Race")
    }), insured,
    {family: "binomial", include_values: false}
)
insured_adjusted.coefficients
```

```biolang
assert gd_no[0] == 1692 && gd_yes[0] == 164 && gd_no[1] == 1653 && gd_yes[1] == 198, "source gender-diabetes counts"
assert abs(gender_diabetes_chi.chi2 - 3.433238629121743) < 0.000000001, "source Yates chi-square"
assert abs(gender_diabetes_fisher.p_value - 0.05992352432232489) < 0.000000000001, "source Fisher p-value"
assert ri_no == [46, 86, 89, 147, 33, 307], "source uninsured counts"
assert ri_yes == [169, 330, 151, 141, 65, 2141], "source insured counts"
assert abs(race_insurance_chi.chi2 - 323.3016487345038) < 0.000000001, "source race-insurance chi-square"
assert abs(race_insurance_chi.p_value - 9.754237575941022e-68) / 9.754237575941022e-68 < 0.000000001, "source race-insurance p-value"
assert insured_by_race.encodings[0].reference == "White", "source White race baseline"
assert abs(insured_by_race.coefficients[0].estimate - 1.9421805410025388) < 0.000000001, "source race-only intercept"
assert abs(insured_by_race.coefficients[1].estimate - -0.6409232225685543) < 0.000000001, "source Asian coefficient"
assert abs(insured_adjusted.coefficients[0].estimate - -0.350140855485075) < 0.000000001, "source adjusted intercept"
assert abs(insured_adjusted.coefficients[3].estimate - -0.4550403000469768) < 0.000000001, "source adjusted Asian coefficient"
"Exact source tables, models, and runtime figures passed."
```

## 8.5 Power & sample size

> **Understanding power calculations**
>
> **Use them when:** planning a study around a pre-specified meaningful effect, alpha, target power, variability, allocation, and analysis method.
>
> **How to read them:** larger samples detect smaller effects more reliably, but the result is only as credible as its assumptions. Recalculate across plausible inputs rather than presenting one optimistic number.

### 8.5.1 T-test power/N

The source uses R's default `strict=FALSE` convention for a two-sided `power.t.test()`: under a specified positive effect, rejection in the opposite tail is not counted as power. BioLang makes that convention explicit.

```biolang
let t_power_20 = power_t_test(2.0 / 2.3, 0.05, nil, {n: 20, strict: false})
t_power_20
```

```biolang
let t_required = power_t_test(0.8 / 1.5, 0.05, 0.8, {strict: false})
t_required
```

The chapter's gene-expression exercises treat a two-fold change as 1 on the log2 scale.

```biolang
let gene_power = power_t_test(1.0 / 0.7, 0.05, nil, {n: 3, strict: false})
let gene_required = power_t_test(1.0 / 0.7, 0.05, 0.8, {strict: false})
{power_with_three_per_group: gene_power, required_for_80_percent: gene_required}
```

### 8.5.2 Proportions power/N

```biolang
let proportion_power = power_prop_test(0.8, 0.2, {n: 5})
let proportion_required = power_prop_test(0.8, 0.2, {power: 0.9})
{power_with_five_per_group: proportion_power, required_for_90_percent: proportion_required}
```

The final exercise plans a GWAS for prevalence changing from 5% to 5.5% at genome-wide alpha (5e-8).

```biolang
let gwas_required = power_prop_test(0.05, 0.055, {power: 0.8, alpha: 5e-8})
gwas_required
```

```biolang
assert abs(t_power_20.power - 0.7641668) < 0.00001, "source fixed-n t-test power"
assert abs(t_required.n_raw - 56.16413) < 0.001, "source t-test required n"
assert abs(gene_power.power - 0.2709095) < 0.00001, "source gene-expression power"
assert abs(gene_required.n_raw - 8.764711) < 0.001, "source gene-expression required n"
assert abs(proportion_power.power - 0.4688159) < 0.00001, "source proportion power"
assert abs(proportion_required.n_raw - 12.37701) < 0.001, "source proportion required n"
assert abs(gwas_required.n_raw - 157589.5) < 2.0, "source GWAS required n"
"Every published section 8.5 calculation passed."
```

## 8.6 Tidying models

This notebook directly ports the `tidy()`, `augment()`, and `glance()` sequence in section 8.6 of [*Biological Data Science with R*, Essential statistics](https://bdsr.stephenturner.us/stats.html). BioLang's diagnostic record already exposes these three views without parsing printed console text.

> **Understanding model diagnostics**
>
> **Use them when:** a fitted model must be checked rather than accepted because it returned coefficients.
>
> **How to read them:** residuals should look like structureless errors around zero. Curvature, funnels, clusters, or isolated influential points indicate that the model, scale, variance, or grouping may need reconsideration.

```biolang
let full_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({
        PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive"),
        Age: adults |> col("Age"),
        Gender: adults |> col("Gender")
    }),
    adults |> col("Testosterone"),
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: true}
)
```

### Tidy: one row per coefficient

BioLang's internal feature order is alphabetical, so this table explicitly presents terms in the source formula order.

```biolang
let coefficients = full_model.coefficients
let tidy_coefficients = table({
    term: ["(Intercept)", "PhysActiveYes", "Age", "Gendermale"],
    estimate: [coefficients[0].estimate, coefficients[3].estimate, coefficients[1].estimate, coefficients[2].estimate],
    std_error: [coefficients[0].standard_error, coefficients[3].standard_error, coefficients[1].standard_error, coefficients[2].standard_error],
    p_value: [coefficients[0].p_value, coefficients[3].p_value, coefficients[1].p_value, coefficients[2].p_value]
})
tidy_coefficients
```

```biolang
tidy_coefficients |> filter(|row| row.term != "(Intercept)") |> select("term", "p_value") |> arrange("p_value")
```

### Augment: fitted values and residuals

The source plots only males, colours points by physical activity, and scales point size by age. BioLang regenerates that same plot from the fitted model's exact complete rows; the source axis and legend labels are preserved.

```biolang
let adult_gender = adults |> col("Gender")
let adult_activity = adults |> col("PhysActive")
let adult_age = adults |> col("Age")
let model_points = full_model.fitted_intervals
let male_model_points = model_points |> filter(|point| adult_gender[point.row] == "male")
let male_fitted = male_model_points |> map(|point| point.fitted)
let male_residuals = male_model_points |> map(|point| point.residual)
let male_activity = male_model_points |> map(|point| adult_activity[point.row])
let male_age = male_model_points |> map(|point| adult_age[point.row])

stats_relationship_plot(male_fitted, male_residuals, {
    group: male_activity, size: male_age, fit: false, interval: "none",
    theme: "ggplot", title: "", x_label: ".fitted", y_label: ".resid",
    legend_title: "PhysActive", size_legend_title: "Age", note: false,
    width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Glance: one model-level row

```biolang
table({
    complete_rows: [full_model.complete_rows],
    excluded_rows: [full_model.excluded_rows],
    degrees_freedom: [full_model.degrees_freedom],
    r_squared: [full_model.r_squared],
    adjusted_r_squared: [full_model.adjusted_r_squared],
    residual_mse: [full_model.residual_mse]
})
```

```biolang
assert abs(coefficients[0].estimate - 46.69313136232011) < 0.000000001, "source intercept"
assert abs(coefficients[3].estimate - 9.274924731642368) < 0.000000001, "source physical-activity coefficient"
assert abs(coefficients[1].estimate - -0.5903616430454974) < 0.000000001, "source age coefficient"
assert abs(coefficients[2].estimate - 385.1989414002692) < 0.000000001, "source male coefficient"
assert abs(coefficients[3].p_value - 0.037711845766758775) < 0.000000000001, "source physical-activity p-value"
assert full_model.complete_rows == 3438 && full_model.excluded_rows == 269, "source complete cases"
assert abs(full_model.r_squared - 0.6968929855756988) < 0.000000001, "source R squared"
assert len(male_model_points) == 1730, "source male complete cases"
"Exact tidy outputs and runtime residual figure passed."
```

## 8.7 Additional topics and recommended reading

The chapter closes with three study-design cautions. They do not introduce another computed result, so this section uses no invented demonstration data.

### 8.7.1 Batch effects

Batch effects are technical variation introduced by reagent lots, technicians, flow cells, processing dates, or similar operational groups. If batch is confounded with the biological condition, a fitted model cannot reliably distinguish the two effects. The source recommends Scherer's discussion of microarray batch effects and Leek et al., [“Tackling the widespread and critical impact of batch effects in high-throughput data”](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3880143/).

### 8.7.2 What is my n?

The number of rows or assay measurements is not necessarily the sample size. The relevant n is usually the number of independently sampled or independently assigned biological units at the level addressed by the scientific question. The source points to Vaux, Fidler, and Cumming, [“Replicates and repeats—what is the difference and is it significant?”](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3321166/).

### 8.7.3 Technical versus biological replicates

Technical replicates repeat a measurement on the same material and help quantify assay precision. Biological replicates are distinct experimental units and support inference about biological variation. Repeating an assay does not create additional independent biological samples.


